          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: நிலா",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "நிலா (nilā) — \"the moon\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Where both sisters took the Sanskrit moon — candran in Malayalam, candruḍu in Telugu — Tamil's everyday moon is its own. நிலா is inherited, and it is the moon as light rather than as a body: nilā is what lies on a courtyard at night, the moonlight and the moon in one word. Tamil has cantiraṉ as well, from the same Sanskrit its sisters took, and saves it for almanacs and for verse."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Let the ஆ at the end run long. It is the length that makes the word."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Three: the space, the day light, and the night one."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
